00,37.582386,0 126.957589,37.582474,0 126.957589,37.582497,0 126.957590,37.582584,0 126.957639,37.582644,0 126.957673,37.582713,0 126.957695,37.582809,0 126.957687,37.582847,0 126.957693,37.582897,0 126.957736,37.582957,0 126.957744,37.582978,0 126.957749,37.583017,0 126.957733,37.583063,0 126.957731,37.583115,0 126.957693,37.583221,0 126.957683,37.583247,0 126.957696,37.583284,0 126.957751,37.583296,0 126.957782,37.583339,0 126.957806,37.583357,0 126.957829,37.583395,0 126.957825,37.583423,0 126.957821,37.583441,0 126.957766,37.583449,0 126.957756,37.583486,0 126.957718,37.583525,0 126.957720,37.583571,0 126.957745,37.583610,0 126.957765,37.583653,0 126.957776,37.583704,0 126.957751,37.583739,0 126.957747,37.583767,0 126.957750,37.583801,0 126.957771,37.583855,0 126.957834,37.583966,0 126.957885,37.584083,0 126.957914,37.584212,0 126.957940,37.584261,0 126.957951,37.584344,0 126.957953,37.584398,0 126.957947,37.584466,0 126.957944,37.584533,0 126.957946,37.584580,0 126.957942,37.584598,0 126.957989,37.584580,0 126.958015,37.584610,0 126.958043,37.584639,0 126.958041,37.584697,0 126.958050,37.584766,0 126.958062,37.584815,0 126.958074,37.584851,0 126.958078,37.584914,0 126.958077,37.584938,0 126.958053,37.584970,0 126.957882,37.584999,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>1. Gyeongbokgung Stn Exit 1 – Start</name><description><![CDATA[Line 3. Walk west along Sajik-ro, the main road, towards the intersection. Station → summit takes about 1 h (1.5 h at a slow pace).<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/21405163?lng=126.9722150&lat=37.5761970&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1574-0F9D58</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.972215,37.576197,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>2. CU – last shop (buy 2 bottles of water)</name><description><![CDATA[Convenience store on the corner of the fork. There is not a single shop from here to the summit, so get at least 2 bottles of water. A sign at the fork points to another path; ignore it, cross the side road and pass the Sajikdan gate instead.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/1875512750?lng=126.9692830&lat=37.5762840&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1631-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.969283,37.576284,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>3. Sajikdan (Sajik Altar) gate</name><description><![CDATA[Pass the gate and follow the stone wall. At the end of the wall turn right: this is where Inwangsan Jarak-gil starts. Easy walking path, not a trail yet.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/32528333?lng=126.9687560&lat=37.5757670&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1598-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.968756,37.575767,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>4. Ungyeong House (운경고택)</name><description><![CDATA[Joseon-era traditional house right after the corner, home of Ungyeong Lee Jae-hyung, a descendant of Prince Inseong. Opens only for occasional exhibitions. From here keep straight at every fork.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/1481885338?lng=126.9668070&lat=37.5750500&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1600-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.966807,37.575050,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>5. Hwanghakjeong archery pavilion</name><description><![CDATA[Tiled roof between the trees: an archery range built in 1898 under Emperor Gojong, with a free traditional archery museum. Looking is fine, photographing the archers is not. Just past it, next to the badminton club, is the LAST TOILET before the mountain.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/17901320?lng=126.9640210&lat=37.5775510&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1598-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.964021,37.577551,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>6. Inwangsan Tiger Statue – trail entrance</name><description><![CDATA[Start of the Beombawi trail. 1.4 km / ~20 min from the station; a taxi to here saves that part. Turn LEFT at the statue into the trail entrance: steep stairs for 2–3 minutes, then the view opens and you are on the Hanyangdoseong fortress wall path. Beombawi is 800 m / ~20 min further. Illegal parking here is strictly enforced.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/1259724769?lng=126.9619780&lat=37.5803800&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1597-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.961978,37.580380,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>7. Viewpoint – fortress wall &amp; Namsan Tower</name><description><![CDATA[Look back: the fortress wall and N Seoul Tower overlap here, with a small observation deck. The YouTuber's favourite angle, not too high. Over the wall to the left you can see Inwangsa temple and the oddly shaped Seonbawi rock, which Jeong Do-jeon deliberately left outside the wall.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/search/37.579873%2C126.959349">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1523-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.959349,37.579873,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>8. Beombawi (Tiger Rock)</name><description><![CDATA[THE photo spot: the fortress wall winding towards Namsan Tower. Famous night-view point; many people climb only to here at sunset. Summit is 400 m further but it is the hardest part: slippery granite and a cliff section, hiking boots recommended. If you are already exhausted, turn back here. Expect a queue on weekends.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/18662680?lng=126.9579190&lat=37.5815750&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1535-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.957919,37.581575,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>9. Inwangsan summit (338.2 m)</name><description><![CDATA[Panorama of Seoul: Namsan and N Seoul Tower, Lotte Tower, Jongno, Gwanghwamun and Gyeongbokgung; Bukhansan behind the Gichabawi ridge. The cliff below is Chimabawi (Skirt Rock), where deposed Queen Dangyeong spread her red skirt in 1506 to greet King Jungjong in the palace. Descent continues on the blue line.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/search/37.584999%2C126.957882">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1523-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.957882,37.584999,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Descent – B course (Choso Bookshop)</name>
<Placemark><name>Descent: summit → Forest Shelter → Choso Bookshop → Seochon (shortest way down)</name><description><![CDATA[2.6 km<br>1. Summit → wall path north → Gichabawi junction<br>2. Follow the Changuimun sign along the wall → fork Suseongdong / Changuimun<br>3. B course: Suseongdong sign, past the Forest Shelter to the top of the boardwalk stairs<br>4. Steep boardwalk stairs down, cross Inwangsan-ro → Choso Bookshop<br>5. Inwangsan Jarak-gil down to Suseongdong Valley (650 m)<br>6. Through Seochon (Ogin-gil, Pirundae-ro, Jahamun-ro 9-gil) → Sejong Village Food Street]]></description><styleUrl>#line-1</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>126.957882,37.584999,0 126.958053,37.584970,0 126.958050,37.585066,0 126.958051,37.585123,0 126.958055,37.585226,0 126.958053,37.585302,0 126.958072,37.585341,0 126.958108,37.585350,0 126.958128,37.585359,0 126.958154,37.585361,0 126.958177,37.585417,0 126.958168,37.585458,0 126.958167,37.585465,0 126.958220,37.585548,0 126.958318,37.585632,0 126.958489,37.585831,0 126.958561,37.585878,0 126.958603,37.585904,0 126.958767,37.586025,0 126.958887,37.586096,0 126.959013,37.586145,0 126.959058,37.586197,0 126.959172,37.586218,0 126.959562,37.586130,0 126.959841,37.586123,0 126.959903,37.586119,0 126.959943,37.586129,0 126.959970,37.586149,0 126.959979,37.586172,0 126.959976,37.586223,0 126.960390,37.586333,0 126.960438,37.586344,0 126.960668,37.586381,0 126.960779,37.586385,0 126.960934,37.586454,0 126.961127,37.586531,0 126.961187,37.586552,0 126.961287,37.586577,0 126.961378,37.586617,0 126.961580,37.586414,0 126.961811,37.586340,0 126.961908,37.586332,0 126.962007,37.586350,0 126.962029,37.586359,0 126.961967,37.586446,0 126.961896,37.586543,0 126.961915,37.586585,0 126.961957,37.586666,0 126.962039,37.586759,0 126.962177,37.586907,0 126.962208,37.586939,0 126.962231,37.586966,0 126.962407,37.587132,0 126.962429,37.587125,0 126.962523,37.587092,0 126.962560,37.587053,0 126.962648,37.586918,0 126.962667,3