<Placemark><name>10. Gichabawi junction – check the signpost</name><description><![CDATA[First fork after the summit. Do NOT take the Gichabawi (Train Rock) direction: it leads to Hongje-dong, the opposite side from Seochon. Look for the Changuimun sign and go that way; Seochon should keep getting closer in the view.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/search/37.586218%2C126.959172">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1597-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.959172,37.586218,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>11. Fork: Suseongdong Valley / Changuimun</name><description><![CDATA[The wall path splits. Straight on along the wall = C course to Changuimun in Buam-dong (facing Bukhansan and Bugaksan; stairs all the way; bus back to Gyeongbokgung Stn from the Yun Dong-ju Literature Museum). The Suseongdong Valley sign = B course, the shortest way down, followed here. The Forest Shelter is right next to this fork.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/search/37.586617%2C126.961378">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1597-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.961378,37.586617,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>12. Inwangsan Forest Shelter (숲속쉼터)</name><description><![CDATA[Healing spot halfway down: a former military guard post turned into a free rest space open to everyone. Easy to miss, follow the sign and go inside. From here it is a short 300 m to Choso Bookshop: very steep but well-maintained stairs, no hiking boots needed.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/1234841693?lng=126.9622610&lat=37.5865540&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1720-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.962261,37.586554,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>13. Choso Bookshop (더숲 초소책방)</name><description><![CDATA[Old guard post rebuilt as a glass-walled café/bookshop that blends into the forest. You can end the hike here and call a taxi, or walk 650 m further down the Jarak-gil to Seochon. Also the starting point of the B course if you climb this way (village bus to Suseongdong Valley, then stairs only).<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/1602048207?lng=126.9642050&lat=37.5859410&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1534-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.964205,37.585941,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>14. Suseongdong Valley bus stop</name><description><![CDATA[Village bus (Jongno 09) back to Gyeongbokgung Stn, or keep walking through Seochon to the food street.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/30949178?lng=126.9642480&lat=37.5820860&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1532-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.964248,37.582086,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>15. Sejong Village Food Street – End (Exit 2)</name><description><![CDATA[Food alley starting at Gyeongbokgung Stn Exit 2, successor of the Geumcheongyo Market that existed since the Goryeo era; officially designated in 2011. The 4 restaurants from the video are in the Restaurants layer.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/34352103?lng=126.9723530&lat=37.5765550&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1661-DB4437</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.972353,37.576555,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Restaurants (Gyeongbokgung Stn)</name>
<Placemark><name>Seochon Gyedanjip (서촌 계단집) – seasonal seafood</name><description><![CDATA[Old-school seafood joint featured on 'Wednesday Food Talk'; sells the season, so the menu changes with the catch and the cooking stays simple. Basic set: mussel soup, carrot, onion, ssamjang. Autumn: bone-in gizzard shad sashimi; wild rock oysters; cuttlefish blanched or steamed; steamed blue crab; order raw shrimp whenever it is on the menu. The kick: finish with 'bada-ramyeon', ramyeon boiled with the shrimp heads and seasonal shellfish, soup like a haemultang. Two rooms across a narrow alley, you fetch drinks from the fridge yourself.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/31694842?lng=126.9714590&lat=37.5764310&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1577-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.971459,37.576431,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Naejadong Chuncheon Dakgalbi (내자동 닭갈비)</name><description><![CDATA[Pleasantly spicy charcoal dakgalbi, not too strong, no queue, relaxed and spacious; a good alternative when the hot spots are overrun with tourists. Fry the marinated chicken on the hotplate, then the highlight: the fried rice at the end. Sides: rice balls you roll yourself with seaweed flakes, and soft steamed egg to balance the heat.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/37391694?lng=126.9709140&lat=37.5764960&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1577-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.970914,37.576496,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kimjin Moksam main branch (김진목삼 본점) – thick samgyeopsal</name><description><![CDATA[Popular pork belly place run by the son of a 30-year butcher's; home-grown vegetables and house-made kimchi from Korean ingredients. Staff grill the meat for you and check the plate temperature. Dip the pork in myeoljeot (fermented anchovy sauce). A bit pricey, register for the waiting list on your phone and expect a long wait; not a place to linger.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/38291003?lng=126.9695190&lat=37.5771460&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1577-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.969519,37.577146,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mokpo Sebal Nakji (목포세발낙지) – spicy stir-fried octopus</name><description><![CDATA[1 minute on foot from Kimjin Moksam, on Pirundae-ro just outside the busy market alley. Fresh live octopus; the nakji-bokkeum is cooked to the right point in the kitchen first so it arrives soft and springy, with a proper smoky fire taste. Moved a few doors a few years ago, same quality.<br><a href="https://map.naver.com/p/entry/place/38275394?lng=126.9695150&lat=37.5775600&placePath=%2Fhome&entry=plt&searchType=place&c=15.00,0,0,0,dh">Open Naver Map</a>]]></description><styleUrl>#icon-1577-4186F0</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.969515,37.577560,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
</Document></kml>
